rities (s.5) and urban development authorities (s.22), each for one area; (b) the Planning Committee of a local authority acting as an area development authority (s.6); (c) a Board of Appeal (s.54\u201361) that only hears appeals against Town Planning Officer decisions on town planning schemes and is dissolved once it has decided an appeal (s.55(2)); and (d) a committee for joint-ownership plots added by later amendment. None of these is a state-level body with a mandate on planning policy or reform. The State Government and the Chief Town Planner do the state-level work themselves, such as approving development plans and town planning schemes; the Act does not create a board for it. Because Part A is NO, Part B cannot score; it is set to NO. I also searched for any Gujarat state town planning or spatial planning board set up by notification and found none. Gujarat's general development-planning bodies, such as the former State Planning Board or GRIT, are not set up under the planning Act and have no spatial planning mandate, so they do not count. Source notes: (1) The linked PDF is the government-printed edition 'as modified upto 31 May 2000'. It is hosted on cmlarr.org, which is not an official government site. I used it because it is the only full-text copy that passed the accessibility check. (2) I also reviewed the current consolidated text on Indian Kanoon, which includes the Gujarat Act 11 of 2014 amendments that add references to the Gujarat Metropolitan Planning Committee Act, 2008. It still has no state planning board. That page failed the accessibility check, so it is not cited. (3) Other copies could not be used: the India Code PDF (tpudact.pdf) timed out; the townplanning.gujarat.gov.in Act page returned 404; and the PRS PDF, which includes amendments up to 2019, opens but is a scan with no readable text. A reviewer may want to confirm against the 2019 consolidated version on PRS or India Code, but none of the amendment titles suggest a state board was 